Maintainer: gutschke <gutschke@users.noreply.github.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13)
Standards-Version: 4.6.2
Homepage: https://github.com/gutschke/ippfix
Rules-Requires-Root: no

Package: ippfix
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, python3 (>= 3.9), python3-venv, ghostscript (>= 10.0),
 openssl, systemd
Recommends: fonts-dejavu-core
Suggests: ippfix-selfbuild (= ${binary:Version})
Description: IPP proxy that removes embedded fonts from print jobs
 Some HP LaserJet Pro printers silently discard a job whose page embeds too
 many fonts: the printer accepts it, reports the job completed, warms up, and
 marks nothing. No error reaches the client and none appears on the panel.
 .
 ippfix presents itself as an ordinary IPP Everywhere printer, converts the
 text of every PDF to vector outlines so that no font program reaches the
 device, and forwards the job on. Text stays vector, so the printer's own
 rasteriser still renders it at full device precision. Everything else -- the
 printer's capabilities, its live status, its job handling -- is relayed
 untouched, and scanning is not affected.
 .
 The network daemon never executes the document converter: documents are
 handed to a separate, per-connection service that has no network at all.
 .
 Configuration is not shipped with any printer defined. Edit
 /etc/ippfix/ippfix.conf before starting the service.

Package: ippfix-selfbuild
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, init-system-helpers (>= 1.54~), python3-venv
Recommends: ippfix, default-mta | mail-transport-agent
Description: unattended rebuild of ippfix's Python environment
 ippfix runs from a Python virtual environment, so the libraries it depends on
 are fixed when that environment is created and are not updated by apt -- unlike
 a distribution package, which picks up a patched library automatically on
 dist-upgrade. A virtual environment also stops working outright when the
 system Python moves to a new minor version, which is exactly what happens
 during a release upgrade.
 .
 This package closes both gaps. It rebuilds the environment whenever the Python
 interpreter changes or a dependency has a newer release (after apt runs, and
 weekly as a backstop), installing only after the result has been verified
 against the live configuration, with automatic rollback and mail on failure.
 .
 The rebuild runs outside the apt transaction, so it can never delay or break
 apt, and a machine with no network simply retries later.
